  "query_preview": "..Southerly flow will continue to persist across the\narea through Thursday with well above normal precipitable water\nvalues (PWATs) for September of 1.2\" to 1.8\" across southern \nArizona. This will keep elevated concerns for heavy rain from\nthunderstorms, with hourly rainfall rates of 1\"-2\" (locally \nhigher), flash flooding and increased responses in already \nsaturated washes, rivers and creeks. WPC continues daily slight \nrisk for excessive rainfall across southeast Arizona with the\npotential that Wednesday may be elevated up a category to \nmoderate risk.\n\nEarly morning water vapor imagery showed deep southerly flow\nacross northern Mexico into AZ/NM between upper low west of Baja\nCA and the western flank of subtropical high over the southern\nPlains. To the north there is the tail end of an upper level\ntrof that extends NE across the central plain into the Great\nLakes. Down to our south there is a developing upper low near\nthe southern tip of Baja that will be our weather maker\ntomorrow. \n\nToday: Morning regional radar (yes Tucson radar is down this\nmorning) showed isolated showers moving Gila county NE into the\nMogollon Rim and a few showers NE of Tucson. Additional weak\nshowers are possible through sunrise. The areal coverage of\nstorms this afternoon/evening will be less than yesterday due \nto no discernible disturbance moving thru. However any storms \nthat do develop will be capable of producing hourly rainfall \nrates of 1\"-2\" leading to localized flash flooding. Chances for \nmore scattered storms with locally heavy rain to continue into \nthe overnight hours are elevated versus previous days. \n\nWednesday: Convective activity will be more widespread than today\nas models are all in pretty much in agreement on a strong upper\nlevel disturbance/short wave, currently south of southern tip \nof Baja, being ejected north out of Sonora MX across southern \nArizona. The dynamics associated with this feature will bring a \nbetter chance of more widespread heavy rainfall with hourly \nrainfall rates of 1\"-2.5\", with some areas possibly seeing over \n3\" of rain. This all leads to increased likelihood for flash \nflooding with WPC having our area on the high end of slight risk\nfor excessive rainfall with the possibility that it could be \nelevated to moderate risk later today. Flash flood watch will \nlikely be considered by us and our neighbors later today. \n\nThursday: PWATs remain elevated but there won't be a disturbance\naround like there will be on Wednesday, thus less areal coverage\nof storms. However, still looking at scattered thunderstorms and\ncan't rule out very localized heavy rainfall from any storms \nthat develop, especially east of Tucson, keeping the risk of \nflash flooding elevated.\n\nFriday through Monday: Drier air pushes into southeast Arizona \nfrom the west as our flow aloft turns westerly. This will limit\nany thunderstorm activity to areas east and south of Tucson with\nsignificantly less chances for heavy rainfall.\n\n8 to 14 day outlooks (Tue Sep 22 to Mon Sep 28)\nTemperature: Leaning above normal, 37-39% chance\nPrecipitation: Leaning above normal, 40-44% chance",
